   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to the requirement to\ncheck the justice center's register of substantiated category one cases of\nabuse or neglect",
    "basePrintNo": "S6394",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6394-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§495 & 424-a, Soc Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Social Services Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6394\n\nSPONSOR: CARLUCCI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the social services law, in relation\nto the requirement to check the justice center's register of substanti-\nated category one cases of abuse or neglect\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThis bill would require additional providers of services to people who\nare vulnerable to abuse or neglect to check the register of substanti-\nated category one cases of abuse or neglect maintained by the Justice\nCenter for the Protection of People with Special Needs (\"Justice\nCenter\") before hiring employees or allowing persons to have regular and\nsubstantial contact with a person with a disability, and would allow all\nproviders required to check such register to re-check it no more than\nonce in any six-month period.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Social Services Law (SSL) § 495(2) and\n(3). A new paragraph (a) is added to subdivision 2 and language is\nincluded to add entities to the list of facilities and providers author-\nized to check the register of substantiated category on e cases of abuse\nor neglect maintained by the Justice Center. These additional entities\nare: overnight, summer and traveling summer day camps for children that\nare subject to article thirteen of the Public Health Law (PHL) and\nhealth care providers listed in PHL § 2899(6) (a). Subdivision 2 is also\namended to clarify that the operators of overnight, summer day and trav-\neling summer day camps, and not state or local officials, are responsi-\nble for checking the SEL for prospective employees.\n\nSection 1 also adds a subparagraph (b) to subdivision 2 that would allow\nall providers required to check such register to re-check it no more\nthan once in any six-month period.\n\nSection 1 of the bill would also amend SSL § 495(3) to include language\nto preclude the additional entities included in subdivision 2 from\nhiring a person who is on the register of substantiated category one\ncases of abuse or neglect for any position in which that person would\nhave the potential for regular and substantial contact with persons to\nwhom they would be providing care.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend SSL § 424-a(7) to clarify that a\nfacility or provider as defined by SSL § 488(4) that is required to\ncheck the register of substantiated category one cases of abuse or\nneglect is not required to conduct a subsequent check of the Statewide\nCentral Register for Child Abuse and Maltreatment maintained by the\nOffice of Children and Family Services if the potential employee is on\nsuch register.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would make it effective one year after it is\nsigned into law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Justice Center is responsible for maintaining a statewide register\nof substantiated category one cases of abuse or neglect, known as the\nStaff Exclusion List (SEL), which contains the names of individuals\nfound responsible for serious or repeated acts of abuse and neglect. A\nperson can only be placed on the list after each allegation is thorough-\nly investigated and substantiated based upon a preponderance of\nevidence. The staff person has an opportunity to appeal the decision\nbefore permanently being placed on the SEL. There are currently approxi-\nmately 474 individuals on the SEL who will be barred from future employ-\nment with any facility or provider agency under the jurisdiction of the\nJustice Center. The SEL has already worked to keep individuals who are\non the list from working with vulnerable populations again. Since 2014,\nthe Justice Center has identified almost 100 individuals who were on the\nSEL who attempted to secure subsequent employment in a setting under the\nJustice Center's jurisdiction. Requiring providers to check the SEL\nprevented these individuals who had already committed serious abuse or\nneglect against a vulnerable person from again working with vulnerable\npersons.\n\nWhile many providers and facilities are required to check the SEL, there\nare additional entities that provide services and care to vulnerable\npopulations that should have access to this information about potential\nemployees. Without access to the SEL, providers are not aware that a\npotential employee may have a history of committing serious acts of\nabuse or neglect against service recipients. This proposed legislation\nwould add nursing homes, home care and long term home health programs,\nall children's camps and all adult care facilities to the list of enti-\nties that must check the SEL.  Currently, only children's camps that\nserve twenty percent or more of children with developmental disabilities\nand adult homes or enriched housing programs that have 80 or more beds\nand in which at least twenty-five percent of the residents are persons\nwith serious mental illness are required to check the SEL. Expanding\naccess to the SEL to additional settings will give these facilities and\nproviders critical information when making decisions about allowing an\nindividual to interact with vulnerable persons. It also would address\nthe concern that persons on the SEL who are currently barred from work-\ning in a facility or provider agency under the Justice Center's juris-\ndiction will instead seek employment at a facility or program providing\nservices to vulnerable individuals that is not required or authorized to\ncheck the SEL. The new language makes it clear that this prohibition on\nhiring is limited to positions in which the employee would have the\npotential for regular and substantial contact with persons to whom they\nwould be providing care.\n\nThe legislation also includes language to authorize subsequent checks of\nthe SEL by employers. Currently, there is no mechanism for a provider or\nfacility to check the SEL once an individual is hired. Often, individ-\nuals who provide services to vulnerable persons work in more than one\nfacility or program. If an individual is substantiated for category one\nabuse or neglect in one setting, his or her other employer may not have\nknowledge of that finding. Allowing subsequent checks of the SEL by\nauthorized entities would provide employers with important information\nabout the individuals working with vulnerable populations. This language\nauthorizing subsequent checks mirrors the language that allows provider\ns to re-check the Statewide Central Register for Child Abuse and\nMaltreatment.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA similar version of this bill was introduced in the 2016 and 2017\nlegislative sessions. This bill includes some technical amendments and\nan extended effective date that was requested by the Department of\nHealth.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill would be implemented within existing resources. It would have\nno impact on local governments:\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided however that section\none of this act shall take effect one year after it shall have become a\nlaw and shall apply to any person who is a prospective employee, admin-\nistrator, consultant, intern, volunteer, or contractor considered for\nany position in which they would have regular and substantial contact\nwith persons to whom they would be providing care, on or after such\ndate.\n",
